<main><section class="hero"><div class="eyebrow">EARTH NEWS · EXPLAINED FROM ABOVE</div><h1>LATEST<br>FROM ORBIT.</h1><p>A manually curated briefing about what is changing on Earth—and what satellites actually measured. This is news through imagery, not an automated headline feed.</p><span class="update">UPDATED 13 OCTOBER 2026</span></section>
<section class="feed"><div class="editor-note"><b>How to read this page:</b> each story separates the event from the observation. Dates describe when the imagery or measurement was acquired, which may differ from the publication date.</div>
<article class="story"><div class="image" style="background-image:url('./latest-images/antarctic-sea-ice.jpg')" role="img" aria-label="Satellite map of Antarctic sea ice extent"></div><div class="copy"><div class="eyebrow">CRYOSPHERE · 12 OCT 2026</div><h2>ANTARCTIC SEA ICE REACHES A LOW MAXIMUM.</h2><p>Antarctic sea ice reached its annual winter maximum at the third-lowest extent in the satellite record. Satellites make this comparison possible by repeatedly mapping the boundary between ice-covered and open ocean.</p><div class="facts"><div><span>OBSERVATION</span><strong>Sea-ice extent</strong></div><div><span>DATA</span><strong>Passive microwave sensors</strong></div></div><a class="source" href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/antarctic-sea-ice-grows-to-its-2026-maximum/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">READ THE NASA SOURCE →</a></div></article>
<article class="story"><div class="image" style="background-image:url('./latest-images/thailand-floods.jpg')" role="img" aria-label="Satellite image of flooding in Thailand"></div><div class="copy"><div class="eyebrow">FLOODING · 9 OCT 2026</div><h2>FLOODWATERS SPREAD ACROSS THAILAND.</h2><p>Heavy rainfall beginning in mid-September inundated broad areas and affected millions of people. Comparing recent observations with normal land and river boundaries helps analysts map where water expanded beyond its usual extent.</p><div class="facts"><div><span>OBSERVATION</span><strong>Flood extent</strong></div><div><span>SENSOR</span><strong>Landsat 9 OLI-2</strong></div></div><a class="source" href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/floodwaters-overwhelm-thailand/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">READ THE NASA SOURCE →</a></div></article>
<article class="story"><div class="image" style="background-image:url('./latest-images/texas-drought.jpg')" role="img" aria-label="Satellite image of agricultural fields in the Texas High Plains"></div><div class="copy"><div class="eyebrow">DROUGHT · 8 OCT 2026</div><h2>TEXAS COTTON COUNTRY RUNS SHORT OF WATER.</h2><p>In the Texas High Plains, drought and pressure on the Ogallala Aquifer are visible through crop condition and irrigation patterns. Satellite measurements help compare plant health and water use across thousands of fields.</p><div class="facts"><div><span>OBSERVATION</span><strong>Crop and water stress</strong></div><div><span>VIEW</span><strong>Multispectral imagery</strong></div></div><a class="source" href="https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/fighting-drought-in-texas-cotton-country/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">READ THE NASA SOURCE →</a></div></article>
